      "characterId": "otis-gulp",
      "fullSpecies": "Pelecanus erythrorhynchos",
      "platform": "facebook",
      "contentType": "post",
      "title": "All the way open",
      "status": "candidate",
      "independentLeadReview": "pending",
      "prose": "Spread one wing over the water and began to open the other. A bill was in the way. Folded it again. Turned. Now the first wing brushed somebody else.\n\nI paddled out until I could open both all the way. The wet feathers pulled apart, and a gust caught underneath them. Had to paddle harder to keep from turning.\n\nStayed wide until I was ready to fold them. Then went back to the others and settled close enough to touch.",
      "altText": "A white pelican stands or floats upright in shallow water with both wings extended, black flight feathers visible and several other white pelicans nearby.",
      "fictionBoundary": "Wing spread, water and nearby conspecifics anchor the scene. Partial opening, brushing, displacement, gust, paddling and return are invented. No takeoff, plunge diving, invisible fish, mate, offspring or documented feather-drying purpose claimed. Source identifies Pelecanus erythrorhynchos; adult plumage is visible, sex unrecorded. Male narrator remains fiction.",
      "visualReview": "Exact original individually opened before prose; no crop, upscale or generated image.",
      "sexAgeEvidence": "Wing spread, water and nearby conspecifics anchor the scene. Partial opening, brushing, displacement, gust, paddling and return are invented. No takeoff, plunge diving, invisible fish, mate, offspring or documented feather-drying purpose claimed. Source identifies Pelecanus erythrorhynchos; adult plumage is visible, sex unrecorded. Male narrator remains fiction."
