      "title": "The second set",
      "status": "candidate",
      "independentLeadReview": "pending",
      "prose": "The snow gave way under my feet. I spread my wings, struck down and got back into the air with snow falling off me.\n\nFrom the branch I could see where I had been. A hollow for my body. Long separate grooves on either side.\n\nWent back and stood beside them. Lowered one wing into the untouched snow, lifted it, then lowered the other. Looked underneath. Every feather had made its own thin place.\n\nThis time I jumped toward the branch before opening my wings. Wanted to leave the second set sharp.",
      "altText": "A deep bird-shaped depression and clearly separated wing-feather impressions mark snow, with other tracks along the right edge; no bird is present.",
      "fictionBoundary": "The NPS record explicitly identifies common raven wing prints. The narrator making the print, failed footing, return and deliberate second impression are fiction. Only one large impression is photographed. This is an aftermath image of the central physical action, not a portrait or proof of an experiment. No universal self-recognition or cognition claim. Source sex and age are unknown; established adult female narrator remains fiction.",
